interviewQuestions.answer:
In C#, every type is either a value type or a reference type, and the difference controls how assignment, copying, and method parameter passing behave.

Value types (int, double, bool, char, struct, enum) store their data directly in the variable. Assigning one value-type variable to another copies the data, producing two independent values. Reference types (class, string, array, object, interface, delegate) store a reference to data on the heap; assigning one reference-type variable to another copies the reference, so both variables point at the same object.

```csharp
struct Point { public int X; }
class Box { public int X; }

Point p1 = new Point { X = 1 };
Point p2 = p1;
p2.X = 99;
Console.WriteLine(p1.X); // 1 -- independent copy

Box b1 = new Box { X = 1 };
Box b2 = b1;
b2.X = 99;
Console.WriteLine(b1.X); // 99 -- same object
```

The practical consequence is aliasing: mutating a reference-type instance through one variable is visible through every other variable referring to the same object, while mutating a value-type variable never affects any other variable.

