## Arithmetic, Comparison, and Logical Operators

C# operators let you combine and compare values to build expressions and control program logic.

Arithmetic operators (+, -, *, /, %) work on numeric types. A critical gotcha is integer division: dividing two int values truncates toward zero, discarding the remainder, while dividing double or decimal values keeps the fractional part. The % (modulo) operator returns the remainder of a division and is commonly used to test for even/odd numbers or to wrap values into a range.

Comparison operators (==, !=, <, >, <=, >=) produce a bool. For value types, == compares the actual values. For most reference types, == compares references (identity) unless the type overrides it -- string is the notable built-in exception, where == compares character content.

Logical operators (&&, ||, !) combine boolean expressions:

- && (logical AND) is true only when both operands are true.
- || (logical OR) is true when at least one operand is true.
- ! negates a boolean.

Both && and || use short-circuit evaluation: if the left operand already determines the result, the right operand is never evaluated. This is not just an optimization -- it is commonly relied on to avoid errors, such as checking an index is in range before using it.

```csharp
int[] data = { 1, 2, 3 };
int index = 5;
if (index < data.Length && data[index] > 0)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Safe access");
}
// index < data.Length is false, so data[index] is never evaluated,
// avoiding an IndexOutOfRangeException.
```

C# also has non-short-circuiting bitwise/logical equivalents, & and |, which always evaluate both operands -- useful for bitwise operations on integers, but easy to misuse accidentally in place of &&/||.

codeExamples.code:
int a = 7;
int b = 2;
double c = 7.0;
double d = 2.0;
Console.WriteLine(a / b);
Console.WriteLine(c / d);
Console.WriteLine(a % b);

int x = 5;
bool result = (x > 0) && (10 / x > 1);
Console.WriteLine(result);
Console.WriteLine(x == 5 || x == 10);

int score = 85;
string grade = score >= 60 ? "Pass" : "Fail";
Console.WriteLine(grade);

string? middleName = null;
string displayName = middleName ?? "N/A";
Console.WriteLine(displayName);

middleName ??= "Kumar";
Console.WriteLine(middleName);

string[]? items = null;
Console.WriteLine(items?.Length ?? 0);

interviewQuestions.answer:
The == operator compiles to different behavior depending on the operand types.

For value types (int, double, struct without an overload, and so on), == compares the actual values, so two variables holding the same number are equal.

For reference types in general, the default == compares reference identity -- whether both variables point to the exact same object in memory -- not whether the objects contain equal data. Two separately created objects with identical field values are not == equal unless the type explicitly overrides the operator.

string is the important exception: although string is a reference type, it overrides == to compare the characters of the two strings, so two different string instances with the same text compare as equal.

```csharp
int a = 5, b = 5;
Console.WriteLine(a == b); // True, value comparison

object o1 = new object();
object o2 = new object();
Console.WriteLine(o1 == o2); // False, different objects

string s1 = "hi";
string s2 = "h" + "i";
Console.WriteLine(s1 == s2); // True, content comparison
```

For custom classes, override Equals (and typically == as well) when value-based equality, rather than reference identity, is the meaningful comparison for that type.

These three operators all deal with null in different situations.

?? (null-coalescing) evaluates to its left operand if that is not null, otherwise it evaluates the right operand. It is used to supply a fallback value:

```csharp
string? input = null;
string value = input ?? "default";
```

??= (null-coalescing assignment, C# 8+) assigns the right-hand value to the left-hand variable only when the variable is currently null, leaving it unchanged otherwise. It is shorthand for x = x ?? y; and is commonly used for lazy initialization or applying a default only once:

```csharp
List<string>? cache = null;
cache ??= new List<string>();
```

?. (null-conditional) accesses a member or calls a method only if the object is not null; if the object is null, the whole expression short-circuits to null instead of throwing a NullReferenceException. It is frequently combined with ?? to supply a final fallback:

```csharp
Customer? customer = GetCustomer();
int orderCount = customer?.Orders?.Count ?? 0;
```

Together these operators replace many verbose null-checking if statements with compact, readable expressions, while still making the null-handling intent explicit at the call site.

&& and || are the logical AND and OR operators with short-circuit evaluation: && only evaluates its right operand if the left operand is true, and || only evaluates its right operand if the left operand is false. & and | are the bitwise/logical AND and OR operators; when applied to bool operands they still produce a boolean result, but they always evaluate both operands regardless of the left side.

```csharp
int[] data = { 1, 2, 3 };
int index = 10;

// Safe: short-circuits before the out-of-range access
if (index < data.Length && data[index] > 0) { }

// Unsafe: both sides are always evaluated, so data[index] still runs
if (index < data.Length & data[index] > 0) { }
```

The second example throws an IndexOutOfRangeException even though the first condition is false, because & does not short-circuit. This matters whenever the right-hand expression has a side effect or depends on the left-hand condition being true for it to be safe to evaluate, such as a null check before a member access, or a boundary check before an indexer. Beyond booleans, & and | also serve as the bitwise AND/OR operators on integer types, which is a separate and equally important use. As a general rule, use && and || for ordinary conditional logic, and reach for & / | only when you specifically need non-short-circuit evaluation or bitwise operations on integers.

codeExamples.code:
string first = "Ada";
string last = "Lovelace";
string full1 = first + " " + last;
string full2 = $"{first} {last}";
Console.WriteLine(full1);
Console.WriteLine(full2);

int qty = 3;
double price = 2.5;
Console.WriteLine($"Total: {qty * price}");
Console.WriteLine($"Upper: {"hello".ToUpper()}");

double amount = 1234.5;
Console.WriteLine(String.Format("{0:C}", amount));
Console.WriteLine($"{amount:N2}");
Console.WriteLine($"{amount:F1}");

string path = @"C:\Users\Ada\Docs";
string multi = @"Line1
Line2";
Console.WriteLine(path);
Console.WriteLine(multi);

interviewQuestions.answer:
Both techniques combine literal text with values into a single string, but they differ in readability and, to a lesser extent, in how the compiler generates code.

Concatenation with + joins strings (and other values via ToString()) end to end:

```csharp
string name = "Ada";
int orders = 3;
string message = name + " has " + orders + " orders.";
```

String interpolation, using the $ prefix, lets you embed expressions directly inside the string literal using curly braces:

```csharp
string message = $"{name} has {orders} orders.";
```

Both produce the same result here, but interpolation scales far better as the number of embedded values grows, since the text and the values stay visually in place rather than being broken up by plus signs and quotes. The compiler translates an interpolated string into an efficient formatting call behind the scenes, so there is no meaningful performance difference for typical use; the real advantage of interpolation is readability and fewer opportunities to introduce a spacing or ordering mistake.

Strings in C# are immutable: once created, the character data of a string instance never changes. Every + concatenation (or interpolation) that produces a new string allocates a brand-new string object and copies the combined characters into it, leaving the original strings untouched and eventually garbage. Inside a loop that runs many times, repeatedly concatenating onto an accumulator string means each iteration allocates and copies an ever-larger string, giving roughly quadratic time and memory churn overall.

StringBuilder solves this by maintaining an internal, resizable character buffer that can be appended to in place, only allocating a new internal array occasionally when it needs to grow, rather than on every single append:

```csharp
var sb = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
{
    sb.Append(i).Append(',');
}
string result = sb.ToString();
```

String interning is a separate optimization: the runtime maintains a pool of unique string literals, so identical string literals appearing anywhere in compiled code can share a single underlying object instead of each allocating its own. String.Intern can be called explicitly to add a runtime-constructed string to that pool, and string.IsInterned checks whether an equal string is already there. Interning trades a lookup cost for memory savings and is most valuable when the same literal text appears extremely often; it does not apply automatically to strings built at runtime through concatenation or interpolation.

