## if, else if, and else

The if statement is the most fundamental branching construct in C#. It evaluates a boolean expression and executes a block of code only when that expression is true.

```csharp
if (temperature > 30)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Hot");
}
```

Adding else provides an alternative branch that runs when the condition is false, and chaining else if lets you test a sequence of conditions in order, stopping at the first one that matches:

```csharp
if (temperature > 30)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Hot");
}
else if (temperature > 15)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Mild");
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Cold");
}
```

Only one branch of an if/else if/else chain ever executes, and the conditions are checked top to bottom -- order matters when ranges overlap conceptually, because the first true condition wins even if a later one would also match.

- Braces { } are optional for single-statement bodies, but omitting them is a common source of bugs when code is edited later and a second statement is accidentally assumed to be part of the branch. Most style guides, including ours, require braces always.
- Conditions can be combined with &&, ||, and ! to express compound logic without nesting.
- Deeply nested if statements usually indicate the logic should be extracted into a method, or that an early-return ("guard clause") style would read more clearly.
- For a single yes/no decision producing a value (not executing a statement), the ternary operator ?: is often clearer than a full if/else.

Mastering if/else is a prerequisite for everything else in this topic: switch statements and switch expressions, covered next, are often better suited once you are choosing between many discrete cases rather than a handful of ranges.

codeExamples.code:
int temp = 75;
if (temp > 90)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Hot");
}
else if (temp > 60)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Warm");
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Cold");
}

int age = 25;
bool hasLicense = true;
if (age >= 18 && hasLicense)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Can drive");
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Cannot drive");
}

