## Switch Statements and Switch Expressions

When a decision depends on matching a single value against many discrete possibilities, a switch is usually clearer than a long if/else if chain.

A switch statement compares an expression against a series of case labels. Each matching case runs until it hits a break (or return/throw), and multiple labels can share one block by stacking them, as with days of the week falling into "weekday" vs "weekend." An optional default case handles anything not explicitly listed.

```csharp
switch (day)
{
    case DayOfWeek.Saturday:
    case DayOfWeek.Sunday:
        Console.WriteLine("Weekend");
        break;
    default:
        Console.WriteLine("Weekday");
        break;
}
```

Since C# 8, a switch expression offers a more compact, value-producing form using => arrows instead of case/break, and _ as the catch-all discard pattern instead of default:

```csharp
string category = score switch
{
    >= 90 => "A",
    >= 80 => "B",
    >= 70 => "C",
    _ => "F"
};
```

Switch expressions support rich pattern matching: relational patterns (>= 90), combinators (and, or, not), type patterns (case Circle c =>), and property patterns (case { Count: 0 } =>), making them useful for far more than simple equality checks. Because a switch expression must produce a value, the compiler also warns if the patterns do not appear exhaustive, nudging you toward handling every case including _.

- Use a switch statement when each branch performs different actions/side effects.
- Use a switch expression when you are computing and returning/assigning a single value.
- Pattern matching in switches often replaces long is/as chains with something far more declarative.

Both forms ultimately compile to efficient jump tables or sequential comparisons depending on the case values, so performance is rarely the deciding factor between them -- readability and intent should guide the choice.

codeExamples.code:
int day = 6;
switch (day)
{
    case 1:
    case 2:
    case 3:
    case 4:
    case 5:
        Console.WriteLine("Weekday");
        break;
    case 6:
    case 7:
        Console.WriteLine("Weekend");
        break;
    default:
        Console.WriteLine("Invalid");
        break;
}

int score = 82;
string grade = score switch
{
    >= 90 => "A",
    >= 80 and < 90 => "B",
    >= 70 and < 80 => "C",
    _ => "F"
};
Console.WriteLine(grade);

interviewQuestions.answer:
A switch is the better fit whenever you are comparing a single value against several discrete, specific possibilities, especially more than two or three of them. It communicates the intent -- "branch based on this one value" -- more directly than a chain of if/else if checks that each repeat the same variable:

```csharp
switch (dayNumber)
{
    case 1: Console.WriteLine("Monday"); break;
    case 2: Console.WriteLine("Tuesday"); break;
    default: Console.WriteLine("Other day"); break;
}
```

An if/else if chain remains the better choice when the conditions are ranges, combinations of multiple variables, or otherwise are not simple equality checks against one value -- although modern switch expressions with relational and pattern-matching cases have narrowed this gap considerably, since they can also express ranges and type checks. In general, reach for switch when the shape of the logic is "one value, many discrete cases," and keep if/else if for compound boolean conditions.

A switch statement (available since C# 1) executes one of several statement blocks based on matching an expression against case labels, using break (or return/throw) to end each case, and optionally a default label for anything unmatched. It does not produce a value itself -- each case performs its own actions.

A switch expression (introduced in C# 8) is itself an expression that evaluates to a value, using => to associate each pattern with a result and _ as the discard/catch-all pattern instead of default:

```csharp
// statement form
switch (grade)
{
    case "A": Console.WriteLine("Excellent"); break;
    default: Console.WriteLine("Other"); break;
}

// expression form
string message = grade switch
{
    "A" => "Excellent",
    _ => "Other"
};
Console.WriteLine(message);
```

Switch expressions are generally more concise when every branch is computing the same kind of value, support rich pattern matching (type patterns, property patterns, relational patterns, and combinators such as and/or/not), and the compiler warns when the patterns do not appear to cover every case. Switch statements remain appropriate when each branch performs different side effects rather than producing a single value, or when fall-through across non-empty cases is genuinely needed, which switch expressions do not support.

