## for, while, and do-while Loops

C# provides three classic loop constructs, each suited to a slightly different scenario.

A for loop is ideal when you know in advance how many iterations you need, or need explicit control over a counter. It bundles initialization, condition, and increment into one line:

```csharp
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
{
    Console.WriteLine(i);
}
```

A while loop checks its condition before each iteration, so the body may run zero times if the condition is false from the start:

```csharp
while (queue.Count > 0)
{
    Process(queue.Dequeue());
}
```

A do-while loop checks its condition after each iteration, guaranteeing the body runs at least once -- useful for things like menu prompts where you always want to show the menu before checking whether to repeat:

```csharp
int attempts = 0;
do
{
    attempts++;
} while (attempts < 3 && !Succeeded());
```

- Choose for when the number of iterations (or the counter logic) is central to the loop.
- Choose while when you are repeating based on a condition that might already be false.
- Choose do-while only when you specifically need the body to execute before the first check -- it is used far less often than the other two.
- All three loops can be exited early with break and can skip to the next iteration with continue (covered in the next lesson).
- An infinite loop (for (;;) or while (true)) is valid and common for event loops or servers, but needs an internal break or return to terminate.

Choosing the right loop construct communicates intent to future readers of the code just as much as it controls execution -- a do-while immediately signals "this always runs once," which a while does not.

codeExamples.code:
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++)
{
    Console.WriteLine(i);
}

int n = 5;
while (n < 3)
{
    Console.WriteLine("while: " + n);
    n++;
}

int m = 5;
do
{
    Console.WriteLine("do-while: " + m);
    m++;
} while (m < 3);

string[] fruits = { "apple", "banana", "cherry" };
foreach (string fruit in fruits)
{
    Console.WriteLine(fruit);
}

for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
{
    if (i == 2)
    {
        continue;
    }
    if (i == 4)
    {
        break;
    }
    Console.WriteLine(i);
}

interviewQuestions.answer:
Both loops repeat a block of code while a condition remains true, but they differ in when that condition is checked.

A while loop checks the condition before each iteration, including the very first one, so the body may execute zero times if the condition is already false when the loop is reached:

```csharp
int n = 10;
while (n < 5)
{
    Console.WriteLine(n); // never runs, condition is false from the start
}
```

A do-while loop checks the condition after each iteration, so the body always executes at least once regardless of the initial condition:

```csharp
int m = 10;
do
{
    Console.WriteLine(m); // runs once, printing 10
} while (m < 5);
```

In practice, while is used far more often, since most repeated logic should be skippable entirely if the condition is already false. do-while is reserved for the specific case where the body genuinely needs to run before the condition is meaningful to check, such as showing a menu once before asking whether to show it again.

foreach is syntactic sugar over the iterator pattern defined by IEnumerable and IEnumerable<T>. The compiler translates a foreach loop into a call to GetEnumerator() on the collection, followed by repeated calls to MoveNext() and reads of the Current property until MoveNext() returns false, with the enumerator disposed afterward (important for enumerators that hold unmanaged resources).

```csharp
// roughly equivalent to:
var enumerator = collection.GetEnumerator();
try
{
    while (enumerator.MoveNext())
    {
        var item = enumerator.Current;
        // loop body
    }
}
finally
{
    (enumerator as IDisposable)?.Dispose();
}
```

Most of the built-in mutable collections, such as List<T> and Dictionary<TKey, TValue>, track a version number that increments on every structural modification (add or remove, though not usually simple value changes to existing elements). Their enumerator checks that version on every MoveNext() call, and throws an InvalidOperationException if it detects the collection changed mid-iteration. This protects against undefined behavior that would otherwise occur from the internal data structure shifting underneath an in-progress enumeration. To mutate while conceptually iterating, iterate over a snapshot copy (for example, foreach (var item in list.ToList())) or collect the changes to apply and perform them after the loop finishes.

For foreach over an array or a List<T>, the C# compiler specializes the generated code: with an array, it compiles down to an ordinary indexed for loop with bounds checking that the JIT can often optimize away; with List<T>, it uses the concrete struct enumerator type that List<T>.GetEnumerator() returns, so the loop avoids any interface dispatch or heap allocation for the enumerator itself. foreach over a plain IEnumerable<T>-typed reference, however, must call through the interface, which boxes a struct enumerator (if any) and prevents some JIT optimizations, making it modestly slower than iterating a concretely-typed array or List<T> directly.

yield return lets you write a method that returns IEnumerable<T> (or IEnumerator<T>) using ordinary loop and branching syntax, while the compiler transforms it into a state machine that produces values lazily, one at a time, only as the consumer actually asks for the next one via foreach:

```csharp
IEnumerable<int> Evens(int max)
{
    for (int i = 0; i <= max; i += 2)
    {
        yield return i;
    }
}

foreach (int n in Evens(6))
{
    Console.WriteLine(n);
}
```

This is called deferred execution: none of the method body runs until the first MoveNext() call from the foreach loop, and execution pauses at each yield return until the next value is requested. This matters for performance and correctness together, since it allows processing huge or infinite sequences without materializing them all in memory at once, but it also means side effects inside an iterator method only happen as the sequence is actually consumed, which can surprise developers who expect it to run eagerly like a normal method.

