## Method Basics, Return Types, and ref/out/in Parameters

A method groups a block of reusable logic behind a name, optional parameters, and a return type. The return type void means the method produces no value; any other type means the method must return a value of that type (or compatible with it) on every code path.

```csharp
int Square(int x)
{
    return x * x;
}
```

By default, arguments are passed by value -- the method receives a copy, so reassigning the parameter inside the method does not affect the caller variable (for reference types, the reference itself is copied, so mutating the object it points to is still visible to the caller, but reassigning the parameter to a new object is not).

Three keywords change this behavior:

- ref passes an existing variable by reference, so changes the method makes to the parameter are visible to the caller. The variable must be initialized before the call.
- out also passes by reference, but is intended for a method to produce a value the caller did not have yet; the variable need not be initialized beforehand, and the method is required to assign it before returning. The classic example is int.TryParse(string, out int).
- in passes a value by reference for efficiency (avoiding a copy of a large struct) while preventing the method from modifying it -- effectively a read-only reference.

```csharp
bool TryDivide(int a, int b, out int result)
{
    if (b == 0) { result = 0; return false; }
    result = a / b;
    return true;
}
```

Use ref/out sparingly -- they make call sites harder to read (mitigated somewhat by requiring ref/out at the call site too) and are best reserved for patterns like TryParse or performance-critical code involving large structs.

codeExamples.code:
void Double(ref int number)
{
    number *= 2;
}

int value = 10;
Double(ref value);
Console.WriteLine(value);

bool success = int.TryParse("123", out int result);
Console.WriteLine(success);
Console.WriteLine(result);

void Greet(string name, string greeting = "Hello")
{
    Console.WriteLine($"{greeting}, {name}!");
}

Greet("Maria");
Greet(name: "Raj", greeting: "Hi");

int Sum(params int[] numbers)
{
    int total = 0;
    foreach (int n in numbers)
    {
        total += n;
    }
    return total;
}

void Print(string s) => Console.WriteLine($"string: {s}");
void Print(int i) => Console.WriteLine($"int: {i}");

Console.WriteLine(Sum(1, 2, 3, 4));
Print("hi");
Print(5);

interviewQuestions.answer:
Both ref and out let a method parameter be passed by reference instead of by value, so changes the method makes to the parameter are visible to the caller, but they differ in initialization requirements and intended usage.

ref requires the caller variable to already be initialized before the call, and the method is free to read it before assigning anything new to it:

```csharp
void Double(ref int number)
{
    number *= 2;
}

int value = 10;
Double(ref value);
Console.WriteLine(value); // 20
```

out does not require the caller variable to be initialized beforehand, but in exchange, the method is required to assign a value to it on every code path before returning. out signals that the parameter exists purely to let the method hand back an additional result, which is the pattern used throughout the .NET TryParse family:

```csharp
bool success = int.TryParse("123", out int result);
Console.WriteLine(success); // True
Console.WriteLine(result);  // 123
```

In short, use ref when the method may read and modify an existing value, and use out when the method exists specifically to produce one or more output values the caller did not already have.

Method overloading lets a class define multiple methods that share a name but differ in their parameter list: the number of parameters, their types, or their order. When a call is compiled, the compiler examines the argument types at the call site and selects the overload whose parameter list is the best match, preferring an exact type match, then the most specific applicable implicit conversion; if more than one overload is equally good and none is strictly more specific, the call is a compile-time ambiguity error rather than a runtime decision.

```csharp
void Print(int value) => Console.WriteLine($"int: {value}");
void Print(double value) => Console.WriteLine($"double: {value}");
void Print(string value) => Console.WriteLine($"string: {value}");

Print(5);      // int: 5
Print(5.0);    // double: 5
Print("five"); // string: five
```

Return type is deliberately excluded from overload resolution because C# allows calling a method purely for its side effects, discarding the return value entirely: Foo(5); is valid whether Foo returns void, int, or string, so the compiler would have no information from the call site alone to decide which overload was intended if two overloads differed only by return type. Allowing that would make method calls ambiguous in exactly the common case where the result is not used.

The in modifier, introduced in C# 7.2, passes an argument by reference like ref, but marks it as read-only: the method receives a reference to the caller variable rather than a copy, yet the compiler prevents the method body from assigning to or otherwise mutating that parameter (except through explicitly mutable members on a mutable struct, which is why in is most valuable with readonly structs).

```csharp
readonly struct Vector3
{
    public readonly double X, Y, Z;
    public Vector3(double x, double y, double z) { X = x; Y = y; Z = z; }
}

double Length(in Vector3 v) => Math.Sqrt(v.X * v.X + v.Y * v.Y + v.Z * v.Z);
```

The motivation is performance for large struct types: passing a large struct by value copies every field on every call, which can be expensive if the struct is big (say, larger than a couple of machine words) and called frequently. Passing it by reference with in avoids that copy while still giving the caller the same guarantee as passing by value would -- the method cannot change the argument -- which ref does not provide. For small structs (like a single int or double) or reference types, the copy avoided by in is negligible or nonexistent, and the indirection it introduces can occasionally make it slightly slower than an ordinary by-value parameter, so in is a targeted optimization rather than a general default.

