## Fields, Properties, and Constructors

A class bundles data (fields) and behavior (methods) together. Fields are variables declared directly in a class; by convention they are usually private and exposed through properties, which wrap access to a value behind get/set accessors and let you add validation or computed logic later without breaking callers.

Auto-implemented properties let you skip writing a backing field explicitly when there is no extra logic needed:

```csharp
class Book
{
    public string Title { get; set; }
    public int PageCount { get; set; }
}
```

The compiler generates a hidden backing field for each auto-property automatically. You can restrict one accessor more than the other -- a common pattern is public string Name { get; private set; }, which lets any code read Name but only the class itself change it.

A constructor is a special method, named after the class, that runs when new creates an instance. It is typically where you validate and assign initial state:

```csharp
class Book
{
    public string Title { get; }
    public int PageCount { get; }

    public Book(string title, int pageCount)
    {
        Title = title;
        PageCount = pageCount;
    }
}

var book = new Book("Clean Code", 464);
```

If you do not declare any constructor, C# supplies a public parameterless one automatically; as soon as you declare any constructor yourself, that implicit one disappears unless you add it back explicitly.

Object initializers (new Book { Title = "..." }) let you set accessible properties right after construction, which is convenient alongside a parameterless or partial constructor, though it cannot set properties with private or missing setters from outside the class.

Properties, not raw public fields, are the idiomatic way to expose class state in C# -- they give you a stable public API even if the internal representation changes later.

codeExamples.code:
class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }

    public Person(string name, int age)
    {
        Name = name;
        Age = age;
    }
}

var p = new Person("Leena", 30);
Console.WriteLine($"{p.Name} is {p.Age}");

class Product
{
    public string Name { get; private set; }
    public decimal Price { get; set; }

    public Product(string name)
    {
        Name = name;
    }
}

var product = new Product("Keyboard") { Price = 49.99m };
Console.WriteLine($"{product.Name}: {product.Price}");

