## Access Modifiers, this, and Static Members

Access modifiers control what other code can see and use. The most common are:

- public -- accessible from anywhere.
- private -- accessible only within the same class (the default for class members if unspecified).
- protected -- accessible within the class and any class that derives from it.
- internal -- accessible anywhere within the same assembly (project), but not from other assemblies.

Choosing the narrowest modifier that still works is good practice: it keeps internal implementation details from leaking into your public API, making future changes safer.

The this keyword refers to the current instance. Its most common use is disambiguating a field from a parameter or local variable that happens to share the same name:

```csharp
class Rectangle
{
    private int width;
    public Rectangle(int width)
    {
        this.width = width; // "this.width" is the field, "width" is the parameter
    }
}
```

this is also used to chain constructors (public Rectangle() : this(1) { }) and, in extension methods, to indicate the type being extended.

Static members (fields, properties, or methods marked static) belong to the type itself, not to any particular instance. There is exactly one copy of a static field, shared across every instance of the class, and you call a static method through the class name rather than an object reference:

```csharp
class Counter
{
    public static int InstancesCreated = 0;
    public Counter() => InstancesCreated++;
}

_ = new Counter();
_ = new Counter();
Console.WriteLine(Counter.InstancesCreated); // 2
```

A whole class can be marked static, which means it can contain only static members and can never be instantiated -- the typical pattern for utility/helper classes like Math or Console itself.

codeExamples.code:
class Point
{
    private int x;
    private int y;

    public Point(int x, int y)
    {
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
    }

    public override string ToString() => $"({x}, {y})";
}

var pt = new Point(3, 4);
Console.WriteLine(pt);

class Counter
{
    public static int Count = 0;

    public Counter()
    {
        Count++;
    }
}

var c1 = new Counter();
var c2 = new Counter();
var c3 = new Counter();
Console.WriteLine(Counter.Count);

interviewQuestions.answer:
A field is a variable declared directly inside a class or struct that stores data. A property is a member that looks like a field from the outside (you read and write it with the same syntax) but is actually backed by one or two accessor methods, get and set, which can contain logic beyond a plain read or write.

```csharp
class Person
{
    private string name; // field

    public string Name    // property
    {
        get => name;
        set => name = value?.Trim() ?? string.Empty;
    }
}
```

Auto-implemented properties, such as public string Name { get; set; }, let you skip writing an explicit backing field and accessor bodies when no extra logic is needed yet; the compiler generates the hidden field for you.

The reason to prefer properties over public fields on a public API is future flexibility and encapsulation: if validation, change notification, lazy computation, or logging is ever needed when a value is read or set, a property can add that logic without changing the member signature or breaking any compiled code that already calls it, whereas a public field exposes the raw storage location directly and cannot gain that logic later without a breaking change to every consumer.

An instance member (field, property, or method without the static keyword) belongs to a particular object created with new, and each instance has its own copy of instance fields. A static member belongs to the type itself rather than to any instance: there is exactly one copy of a static field shared by the whole application, and a static method is called through the class name rather than through an object reference.

```csharp
class Counter
{
    public static int InstancesCreated = 0; // shared by all instances
    public int Id;                          // unique per instance

    public Counter()
    {
        InstancesCreated++;
        Id = InstancesCreated;
    }
}
```

An entire class can be declared static, which means it can contain only static members and can never be instantiated with new. This is the right design when a class exists purely to group a set of stateless utility operations that do not need or benefit from per-instance state, such as Math or Console in the .NET base class library, or a custom collection of pure helper/formatting functions. If a class needs to track per-instance state, be passed around as an object, support polymorphism (static members cannot be virtual or overridden), or be mocked for testing, it should remain a regular instantiable class instead.

Constructor chaining with : this(...) lets one constructor call another constructor of the same class before running its own body, which avoids duplicating initialization logic across multiple constructors:

```csharp
class Rectangle
{
    public int Width { get; }
    public int Height { get; }

    public Rectangle(int width, int height)
    {
        Width = width;
        Height = height;
    }

    public Rectangle(int side) : this(side, side) { }
}
```

When an object is constructed, C# follows a fixed order, from the top of the hierarchy down:

1. The base class field initializers run.
2. The base class constructor body runs (after first resolving its own : base(...) or : this(...) chain, if any).
3. The derived class field initializers run.
4. The derived class constructor body runs.

If a constructor uses : this(...) to chain to another constructor in the same class, that target constructor (and its own base/this chain) fully completes, including the target constructor body, before control returns to continue the body of the original constructor (which, after the this(...) chain, typically has little or nothing left to do). This matters in practice because a virtual method called from inside a base constructor body can end up running against a derived instance whose own field initializers and constructor body have not executed yet, since step 3 and step 4 happen after step 2 -- a classic source of subtle bugs when a base constructor calls an overridable method expecting derived state to already be set up.

