## base/derived Classes, virtual/override, and sealed

Inheritance lets one class (the derived class) build on another (the base class), reusing and extending its members:

```csharp
class Animal
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public virtual string Speak() => "Some sound";
}

class Dog : Animal
{
    public override string Speak() => "Woof!";
}
```

A member must be declared virtual (or abstract) in the base class to be overridable; the derived class then uses override to provide its own implementation. This is the mechanism behind polymorphism: calling Speak() through a base-typed reference still runs the derived class version at runtime, because the call is resolved dynamically (virtual dispatch) rather than based on the variable compile-time type.

Inside an override, base.MemberName(...) calls the base class implementation explicitly -- useful when the derived behavior should extend rather than fully replace the base behavior:

```csharp
public override string Speak() => base.Speak() + " (a dog sound)";
```

A derived class constructor can forward arguments to the base constructor with : base(...), ensuring the base part of the object is initialized correctly before the derived constructor own body runs.

The sealed modifier prevents further overriding or inheritance:

- sealed override void Method() stops any further-derived class from overriding that specific member again.
- sealed class Foo prevents any class from inheriting from Foo at all.

Sealing is a deliberate design decision: it documents that a class or member behavior is complete and should not be extended, and it also lets the JIT compiler skip virtual-dispatch overhead for calls it can prove are final.

C# only supports single inheritance for classes -- a class can have exactly one base class (implicitly object if none is specified) -- though a class can implement any number of interfaces, discussed in a later topic.

codeExamples.code:
class Animal
{
    public virtual string Speak() => "...";
}

class Dog : Animal
{
    public override string Speak() => base.Speak() + "Woof!";
}

Animal a = new Dog();
Console.WriteLine(a.Speak());

class Base
{
    public virtual void Show() => Console.WriteLine("Base");
}

class Middle : Base
{
    public sealed override void Show() => Console.WriteLine("Middle");
}

class Leaf : Middle
{
    // Cannot override Show() here because Middle sealed it
    public void Describe() => Console.WriteLine("Leaf describes itself");
}

Base b = new Leaf();
b.Show();

