## Abstract Classes, Polymorphism, and is/as Casting

An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly; it exists to be a base class that defines a partial contract. It can mix fully-implemented members with abstract members that have no body and must be implemented by any concrete derived class:

```csharp
abstract class Shape
{
    public abstract double Area();
    public void Describe() => Console.WriteLine($"Area: {Area()}");
}

class Square : Shape
{
    public double Side { get; set; }
    public override double Area() => Side * Side;
}
```

This is polymorphism in action: code written against the abstract Shape type (e.g., a List<Shape>) can hold any mix of concrete derived instances, and calling Area() on each one invokes the correct derived implementation automatically, without the calling code needing to know which concrete type it is dealing with.

When you have a value typed as a base class or object and need to work with it as a more specific derived type, C# gives you two safe casting tools instead of a raw cast that risks an InvalidCastException:

- is tests whether an object is compatible with a type and returns a bool; the pattern-matching form (if (obj is Square sq)) tests and assigns the casted value to a new variable in one step.
- as attempts a cast and returns null instead of throwing if it fails (only valid for reference types and nullable value types).

```csharp
object item = GetItem();
if (item is Square square)
{
    Console.WriteLine(square.Area());
}

Circle? maybeCircle = item as Circle;
if (maybeCircle is null)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Not a circle");
}
```

Prefer is with pattern matching or as over a direct cast ((Square)item) whenever the type is not guaranteed, since a direct cast throws immediately on a mismatch while these alternatives let you handle the mismatch gracefully.

codeExamples.code:
abstract class Shape
{
    public abstract double Area();
}

class Circle : Shape
{
    private readonly double radius;
    public Circle(double radius) => this.radius = radius;
    public override double Area() => Math.PI * radius * radius;
}

class Square : Shape
{
    private readonly double side;
    public Square(double side) => this.side = side;
    public override double Area() => side * side;
}

Shape[] shapes = { new Circle(2), new Square(3) };
foreach (Shape s in shapes)
{
    Console.WriteLine(s.Area().ToString("F2"));
}

object obj = "hello";
if (obj is string s)
{
    Console.WriteLine(s.ToUpper());
}

object number = 42;
string? asString = number as string;
Console.WriteLine(asString ?? "cast failed");

interviewQuestions.answer:
Inheritance lets one class, the derived class, reuse and extend the members of another class, the base class, expressed with a colon after the derived class name:

```csharp
class Animal
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public virtual string Speak() => "...";
}

class Dog : Animal
{
    public override string Speak() => "Woof!";
}
```

A Dog automatically has a Name property and a Speak method inherited from Animal, without redeclaring them, and can add its own members or override inherited ones that are marked virtual or abstract.

The base keyword lets derived-class code explicitly reach the base class version of a member, which is especially useful inside an override when the derived behavior should extend rather than fully replace the base behavior:

```csharp
class Puppy : Dog
{
    public override string Speak() => base.Speak() + " (puppy voice)";
}
```

base is also used to call a specific base class constructor from a derived class constructor, using : base(...) after the constructor parameter list, ensuring the inherited part of the object is properly initialized before the derived constructor body runs. C# allows a class to inherit from only one base class directly (every class ultimately derives from object), though a class can implement multiple interfaces alongside that single base class.

override requires the base member to be declared virtual (or abstract), and it replaces the base implementation for every call made through any reference, whether the reference variable is typed as the base class or the derived class, because override participates in virtual dispatch resolved at runtime based on the actual object type.

new, by contrast, does not require the base member to be virtual, and it does not participate in virtual dispatch at all: it simply declares an unrelated method that happens to share a name, hiding the base version only when accessed through a variable typed as the derived class. Accessed through a variable typed as the base class, the original base implementation still runs, even if the actual object is a derived instance.

```csharp
class Base
{
    public virtual void Greet() => Console.WriteLine("Base greet");
}

class OverrideChild : Base
{
    public override void Greet() => Console.WriteLine("Override greet");
}

class NewChild : Base
{
    public new void Greet() => Console.WriteLine("New greet");
}

Base a = new OverrideChild();
a.Greet(); // "Override greet" -- virtual dispatch

Base b = new NewChild();
b.Greet(); // "Base greet" -- new does not override, base reference sees base method
```

In almost all real designs, override is what you want when extending polymorphic behavior; new is rarely appropriate and usually indicates either a naming collision that should be resolved differently, or a genuine, deliberate case of introducing an unrelated method that intentionally shadows the base one for code accessed through the derived type specifically.

Virtual dispatch is the mechanism that lets a call to a virtual (or abstract, or interface) method resolve to the correct overriding implementation at runtime, based on the actual runtime type of the object, rather than the compile-time type of the variable or expression used to call it. Internally, the runtime achieves this by giving each type a method table (sometimes called a vtable): a virtual method call looks up the method table for the object actual type and invokes whichever implementation is registered there, which is the most-derived override present for that type.

```csharp
class Base
{
    public Base() => Initialize();
    protected virtual void Initialize() => Console.WriteLine("Base Initialize");
}

class Derived : Base
{
    private readonly string label = "configured";
    protected override void Initialize() => Console.WriteLine($"Derived Initialize: {label}");
}

var d = new Derived();
```

Constructing a Derived runs the base class constructor first, which calls the virtual Initialize method; because of virtual dispatch, this invokes the override defined in Derived, not the implementation in Base, even though the Derived field initializers and constructor body have not run yet at that point. The output is "Derived Initialize: " with an empty label, not "configured", because the label field initializer has not executed yet. This is a classic, hard-to-find bug: the code appears to call an overridden method correctly, but the derived-class state it depends on is not ready yet, since base class construction always completes its own constructor body (including any virtual calls it makes) before derived class field initializers and constructor body run. The general guidance is to avoid calling virtual members from within a constructor whenever the override might depend on derived-class state.

codeExamples.code:
interface IGreeter
{
    string Greet(string name);
}

class FormalGreeter : IGreeter
{
    public string Greet(string name) => $"Good day, {name}.";
}

IGreeter greeter = new FormalGreeter();
Console.WriteLine(greeter.Greet("Dr. Rao"));

interface IShape
{
    double Area();
}

class Rectangle : IShape
{
    public double Width { get; set; }
    public double Height { get; set; }
    public double Area() => Width * Height;
}

IShape shape = new Rectangle { Width = 4, Height = 5 };
Console.WriteLine(shape.Area());

