## Multiple Interfaces, Interface vs Abstract Class, and Default Methods

Unlike class inheritance, which is limited to a single base class, a class can implement any number of interfaces:

```csharp
interface IFlyable { void Fly(); }
interface ISwimmable { void Swim(); }

class Duck : IFlyable, ISwimmable
{
    public void Fly() => Console.WriteLine("Flying");
    public void Swim() => Console.WriteLine("Swimming");
}
```

This is how C# achieves the benefits of "multiple inheritance" for behavior contracts without the ambiguity problems that come from inheriting implementation from multiple base classes.

Interface vs abstract class -- a common point of confusion:

- An abstract class can hold shared state (fields) and partially-implemented behavior that derived classes inherit for free; an interface traditionally could not hold instance state at all.
- A class can inherit from only one (abstract or concrete) base class, but can implement many interfaces -- so interfaces are the right tool when unrelated types need to share a contract (e.g., both a File and a NetworkStream might implement IDisposable).
- Use an abstract class when types share a strong "is-a" relationship and meaningful common implementation; use an interface when you are describing a capability that many unrelated types might have.

Since C# 8, interfaces can include default interface methods -- a method with a body, directly in the interface:

```csharp
interface ILogger
{
    void Log(string message) => Console.WriteLine($"[LOG] {message}");
}
```

A class implementing ILogger inherits this default behavior automatically and only needs to override it if it wants different behavior. This feature exists mainly to let library authors add new members to a published interface without breaking every existing implementer -- a form of safe API evolution -- rather than to replace abstract classes for everyday modeling.

codeExamples.code:
interface IFlyable
{
    void Fly();
}

interface ISwimmable
{
    void Swim();
}

class Duck : IFlyable, ISwimmable
{
    public void Fly() => Console.WriteLine("Duck flies");
    public void Swim() => Console.WriteLine("Duck swims");
}

var duck = new Duck();
duck.Fly();
duck.Swim();

interface ILogger
{
    void Log(string message) => Console.WriteLine($"[LOG] {message}");
}

class ConsoleLogger : ILogger
{
    // Uses the interface default implementation
}

ILogger logger = new ConsoleLogger();
logger.Log("Application started");

interviewQuestions.answer:
An interface declares a contract, a set of members a type promises to provide, without (traditionally) supplying any implementation of its own. Any class or struct can implement an interface by providing concrete bodies for every member it declares, and by convention interface names start with a capital I:

```csharp
interface INotifier
{
    void Send(string message);
}

class EmailNotifier : INotifier
{
    public void Send(string message) => Console.WriteLine($"Email: {message}");
}
```

Depending on an interface instead of a concrete class means code can be written once against the interface type and work with any implementation, present or future, without any changes:

```csharp
void Alert(INotifier notifier, string message) => notifier.Send(message);

Alert(new EmailNotifier(), "Build failed");
```

If Alert instead took a concrete EmailNotifier parameter directly, adding a second notification channel, such as an SMS or Slack notifier, would require either duplicating the method or changing its signature. Depending on interfaces rather than concrete classes is also what makes unit testing with fake or mock implementations straightforward, and is the foundation of dependency injection, where a consuming class declares which interfaces it needs and the concrete implementation is supplied from outside.

Both abstract classes and interfaces define a contract that other types must fulfill, but they differ in what else they can provide and how many a single type can use.

An abstract class can hold instance state (fields), fully-implemented methods that derived classes inherit for free, and partially-defined behavior through abstract members that each derived class must implement. A class can inherit from only one base class, abstract or not, so choosing an abstract class commits every derived type to that one hierarchy.

An interface traditionally holds no instance state and, until C# 8, no implementation at all, only member signatures, but a class can implement any number of interfaces simultaneously, letting unrelated types share a contract without being forced into the same inheritance hierarchy.

```csharp
abstract class Shape          // strong is-a relationship, shared state/logic
{
    public abstract double Area();
    public void Describe() => Console.WriteLine($"Area: {Area()}");
}

interface IDisposable          // unrelated types sharing one capability
{
    void Dispose();
}
```

As a rule of thumb: choose an abstract class when a family of types shares a strong is-a relationship and meaningful common implementation (for example, a base Shape for Circle and Square), and choose an interface when you are describing a capability that many otherwise-unrelated types might have (for example, IDisposable, IComparable, or a custom IAuditable that both a UserAccount and an Invoice could implement).

Default interface methods let an interface member include a body directly in the interface declaration, which implementing classes inherit automatically unless they choose to override it:

```csharp
interface ILogger
{
    void Log(string message) => Console.WriteLine($"[LOG] {message}");
}

class ConsoleLogger : ILogger { } // uses the default Log implementation
```

They were introduced primarily to solve an API evolution problem: before C# 8, adding any new member to a published interface was a breaking change, because every existing implementing class would fail to compile until it added the new member. A default implementation lets library authors add new interface members in a later version while existing implementers continue to compile and behave reasonably by inheriting the default, opting in to a custom implementation only when they need different behavior.

If a class implements two interfaces that each declare a default implementation for a method with the same signature, there is a genuine ambiguity (sometimes called the diamond problem), and C# requires the implementing class to resolve it explicitly: the class must provide its own implementation of that method, because neither default is automatically preferred over the other.

```csharp
interface IA { void Greet() => Console.WriteLine("A"); }
interface IB { void Greet() => Console.WriteLine("B"); }

class C : IA, IB
{
    public void Greet() => Console.WriteLine("C resolves the conflict");
}
```

Default interface methods are best understood as a targeted tool for safe API versioning in libraries, not as a general substitute for abstract classes, since interfaces still cannot hold instance fields and the feature intentionally forces explicit resolution whenever a genuine conflict arises.

codeExamples.code:
int[] numbers = new int[] { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
string[] names = ["Alice", "Bob", "Charlie"]; // C# 12 collection expression

Console.WriteLine($"Numbers length: {numbers.Length}");
foreach (var name in names)
{
    Console.WriteLine(name);
}

var fruits = new List<string> { "Apple", "Banana" };
fruits.Add("Cherry");
fruits.Remove("Banana");

Console.WriteLine($"Count: {fruits.Count}");
Console.WriteLine($"Contains Apple: {fruits.Contains("Apple")}");
foreach (var fruit in fruits)
{
    Console.WriteLine(fruit);
}

interviewQuestions.answer:
`List<T>` is backed by a plain array (its `Capacity`). When `Add()` is called and `Count == Capacity`, the list allocates a **new array** — typically double the current capacity — copies all existing elements into it, and discards the old array. This is called **amortized O(1)** insertion: most `Add` calls are O(1), but occasionally one costs O(n) for the copy, and averaged over many additions the cost per Add stays constant.

```csharp
var list = new List<int>(); // Capacity starts small (often 0, then 4)
for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++)
{
    list.Add(i); // occasional internal resize + copy
}
```

The repeated allocation and copying does create extra garbage for the GC to collect and temporarily doubles memory usage during a resize. If you know the approximate final size up front, you can avoid this entirely:

```csharp
var list = new List<int>(1_000_000); // pre-allocate capacity
```

Passing an initial capacity to the constructor tells `List<T>` to allocate that much space immediately, so `Add` calls within that capacity never trigger a resize. This is a common optimization in performance-sensitive code (e.g. parsing large files, hot loops) where you can estimate the number of elements ahead of time.

codeExamples.code:
var ages = new Dictionary<string, int>
{
    ["Alice"] = 30,
    ["Bob"] = 25
};
ages["Charlie"] = 35;

if (ages.TryGetValue("Bob", out int bobAge))
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Bob is {bobAge} years old");
}

Console.WriteLine($"Contains Dave: {ages.ContainsKey("Dave")}");

var stock = new Dictionary<string, int>
{
    ["Widgets"] = 10,
    ["Gadgets"] = 5
};

foreach (KeyValuePair<string, int> item in stock)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"{item.Key}: {item.Value}");
}

// Safe lookup instead of stock["Sprockets"] which would throw KeyNotFoundException
int sprocketCount = stock.TryGetValue("Sprockets", out int count) ? count : 0;
Console.WriteLine($"Sprockets: {sprocketCount}");

interviewQuestions.answer:
Use `TryGetValue`, which returns a `bool` indicating whether the key was found and supplies the value through an `out` parameter rather than throwing:

```csharp
var ages = new Dictionary<string, int> { ["Alice"] = 30 };

if (ages.TryGetValue("Bob", out int age))
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Bob is {age}");
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Bob not found");
}
```

An alternative is checking `ContainsKey` first and then indexing, but that performs two lookups instead of one, so `TryGetValue` is both safer and more efficient. You can also use `GetValueOrDefault(key, fallback)` when you just want a default value instead of branching logic:

```csharp
int age = ages.GetValueOrDefault("Bob", 0);
```

Directly indexing with `dictionary[key]` should be reserved for cases where you are certain the key exists, such as immediately after adding it.

A dictionary key type must provide a consistent way to compute a hash code and determine equality, because `Dictionary<TKey, TValue>` uses `GetHashCode()` to bucket entries and `Equals()` to resolve collisions within a bucket.

The contract requires that:

- If two objects are equal according to `Equals()`, they **must** return the same value from `GetHashCode()`.
- The hash code for an object must not change while it is used as a key (so mutable objects are risky keys).

If you override `Equals()` but leave the default reference-based `GetHashCode()` in place (or vice versa), you break this contract. Two keys that you consider equal might land in different hash buckets, so the dictionary will never find a match for one using the other, causing lookups to silently fail (`TryGetValue` returns `false`) even though an 'equal' entry exists.

```csharp
public class Point
{
    public int X, Y;
    public override bool Equals(object? obj) =>
        obj is Point p && p.X == X && p.Y == Y;
    // Missing GetHashCode() override here is a bug:
    public override int GetHashCode() => HashCode.Combine(X, Y);
}
```

For record types, the compiler generates both `Equals` and `GetHashCode` consistently for you, which is one reason records are a convenient choice for dictionary keys.

Internally, `Dictionary<TKey, TValue>` maintains an array of 'buckets.' When you insert a key, its `GetHashCode()` value is computed and reduced (via modulo) to select a bucket index. Each bucket can hold multiple entries — when two different keys hash to the same bucket (a **collision**), .NET resolves it by chaining: entries in the same bucket are linked together, and a lookup walks that chain comparing keys with `Equals()` until it finds a match.

In the ideal case, hash codes are well-distributed, so each bucket holds roughly one entry and lookups are O(1) on average. A poor `GetHashCode()` implementation — for example, one that always returns the same constant, or one with a narrow range that causes many keys to collide — degrades performance toward **O(n)**, because every lookup has to walk a long chain within the same bucket.

```csharp
// Bad: every instance hashes the same, forcing worst-case collisions
public override int GetHashCode() => 1;

// Good: combines fields so values with different data spread across buckets
public override int GetHashCode() => HashCode.Combine(Id, Region);
```

.NET's `HashCode.Combine` utility is the recommended way to build a well-distributed hash from multiple fields, since it mixes the inputs rather than just summing or XOR-ing them naively (naive XOR combinations can also produce poor distributions for structurally similar objects).

codeExamples.code:
var numbers = new List<int> { 5, 3, 8, 1, 9, 2 };

var result = numbers
    .Where(n => n > 2)
    .OrderBy(n => n)
    .Select(n => n * 10)
    .ToList();

Console.WriteLine(string.Join(", ", result));

int firstBig = numbers.FirstOrDefault(n => n > 100);
Console.WriteLine($"First big: {firstBig}");

var words = new List<string> { "apple", "banana", "avocado", "blueberry", "cherry" };

var groups = words.GroupBy(w => w[0]);
foreach (var group in groups)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"{group.Key}: {string.Join(", ", group)}");
}

int totalLength = words.Aggregate(0, (sum, w) => sum + w.Length);
Console.WriteLine($"Total length: {totalLength}");

var numbers = new List<int> { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 };

var evenSquares = from n in numbers
                   where n % 2 == 0
                   select n * n;

numbers.Add(8); // added before enumeration - deferred execution picks it up

foreach (var sq in evenSquares)
{
    Console.WriteLine(sq);
}

interviewQuestions.answer:
Most LINQ operators that return `IEnumerable<T>` (`Where`, `Select`, `OrderBy`, `GroupBy`, and the query-syntax equivalents) use **deferred execution**: calling them doesn't actually run the query — it just builds up a description of what to do. The query only executes when you actually enumerate it, such as with `foreach`, or when you call something that forces enumeration like `ToList()`, `ToArray()`, `Count()`, or `First()`.

```csharp
var numbers = new List<int> { 1, 2, 3 };
var query = numbers.Where(n => n > 1); // not executed yet

numbers.Add(4);

foreach (var n in query) // executes NOW, sees the added 4
{
    Console.WriteLine(n); // prints 2, 3, 4
}
```

This is powerful for composing queries efficiently and for working with potentially infinite or expensive-to-produce sequences, but it means the data the query operates over is whatever the source collection looks like **at enumeration time**, not at the time the query was written. If the source is later mutated — items added, removed, or changed — a query that's enumerated multiple times can return different results each time, which surprises developers who expect a query to be a one-time snapshot.

To capture a snapshot instead, force immediate execution with `.ToList()` or `.ToArray()` right after building the query.

