## Designing a Custom Exception

A custom exception is a class that derives (directly or indirectly) from `System.Exception`. You create one when a failure represents a distinct, meaningful concept in your application's domain and you want callers to be able to catch it specifically and access extra data about what went wrong.

```csharp
public class InsufficientFundsException : Exception
{
    public decimal ShortfallAmount { get; }

    public InsufficientFundsException(string message, decimal shortfallAmount)
        : base(message)
    {
        ShortfallAmount = shortfallAmount;
    }
}
```

Notice two things here:

- The constructor calls `base(message)` to populate the inherited `Message` property, exactly like any other `Exception`.
- It adds a custom property, `ShortfallAmount`, that carries structured information a generic exception couldn't — callers can read `ex.ShortfallAmount` directly instead of trying to parse it out of a message string.

You throw and catch a custom exception exactly like a built-in one:

```csharp
throw new InsufficientFundsException("Not enough funds.", 50m);
```

By convention, custom exception class names end in `Exception`, and the class should remain focused on representing one specific kind of failure rather than becoming a dumping ground for unrelated error conditions. Keeping exceptions narrowly scoped to a real domain concept keeps catch blocks meaningful and your error-handling code self-documenting.

codeExamples.code:
public class InsufficientFundsException : Exception
{
    public decimal ShortfallAmount { get; }

    public InsufficientFundsException(string message, decimal shortfallAmount)
        : base(message)
    {
        ShortfallAmount = shortfallAmount;
    }
}

void Withdraw(decimal balance, decimal amount)
{
    if (amount > balance)
    {
        throw new InsufficientFundsException(
            "Not enough funds to complete withdrawal.", amount - balance);
    }
    Console.WriteLine($"Withdrew {amount}");
}

try
{
    Withdraw(100m, 150m);
}
catch (InsufficientFundsException ex)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"{ex.Message} Short by {ex.ShortfallAmount}");
}

