## Writing Code Once for Many Types

Generics let you write a class or method that works with **any type**, decided at the point of use, while still getting full compile-time type checking — no casts, and no risk of putting the wrong type in by mistake. A type parameter (conventionally named `T`) acts as a placeholder that's filled in with a concrete type when the generic is used.

```csharp
public class Box<T>
{
    public T Value { get; set; }
    public Box(T value) => Value = value;
}

var intBox = new Box<int>(42);
var textBox = new Box<string>("hello");
```

Generic **methods** work the same way, and the type parameter can often be inferred from the arguments without stating it explicitly:

```csharp
T Max<T>(T a, T b) where T : IComparable<T>
    => a.CompareTo(b) >= 0 ? a : b;

int bigger = Max(3, 7);           // T inferred as int
string later = Max("ant", "bee"); // T inferred as string
```

Without generics, you'd either need a separate `Box` class (and a separate `Max` method) for every type you care about, or fall back to `object`, losing type safety and paying a boxing cost for value types. Generics give you the reusability of `object`-based code with the safety and performance of type-specific code — which is exactly why virtually every built-in .NET collection (`List<T>`, `Dictionary<TKey,TValue>`, `Queue<T>`) is generic.

codeExamples.code:
public class Box<T>
{
    public T Value { get; set; }

    public Box(T value) => Value = value;

    public void Print() => Console.WriteLine($"Box contains: {Value}");
}

T Max<T>(T first, T second) where T : IComparable<T>
{
    return first.CompareTo(second) >= 0 ? first : second;
}

var intBox = new Box<int>(42);
var stringBox = new Box<string>("hello");
intBox.Print();
stringBox.Print();

Console.WriteLine(Max(10, 20));
Console.WriteLine(Max("apple", "banana"));

