## Constraining What T Can Be

By default, a type parameter `T` could be absolutely anything, so the compiler only lets you treat it as `object` inside the generic code — no calling `.CompareTo()`, no `new T()`, nothing beyond what every single type supports. **Constraints** (the `where` clause) narrow down what `T` is allowed to be, in exchange for unlocking more capabilities inside the generic code.

```csharp
public class Repository<T> where T : class, IEntity, new()
{
    public T CreateNew() => new T();       // legal because of new()
    public bool IsValid(T item) => item.Id > 0; // legal because of IEntity
}
```

Common constraints include `where T : class` (reference types only), `where T : struct` (value types only), `where T : SomeBaseClass`, `where T : ISomeInterface`, and `where T : new()` (requires a public parameterless constructor).

### Why Not Just Use object?

Compare the generic approach to an `object`-based one:

```csharp
// object-based: no compile-time safety, boxing for value types
object Add(object a, object b) => (int)a + (int)b; // runtime cast, can throw

// generic: compile-time safety, no boxing for value types
T Add<T>(T a, T b) where T : INumber<T> => a + b;
```

The generic version catches type mistakes at compile time instead of throwing `InvalidCastException` in production, and for value types like `int` or `double`, it avoids the heap allocation that boxing into `object` would require. This combination — safety plus performance — is why generics, not `object`, are the standard way to write reusable collections and algorithms in modern C#.

codeExamples.code:
public interface IEntity
{
    int Id { get; set; }
}

public class Product : IEntity
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
}

public class Repository<T> where T : class, IEntity, new()
{
    private readonly List<T> _items = new();

    public T CreateNew()
    {
        var item = new T();
        _items.Add(item);
        return item;
    }

    public int Count => _items.Count;
}

var repo = new Repository<Product>();
repo.CreateNew();
repo.CreateNew();

Console.WriteLine($"Products created: {repo.Count}");

// No boxing: a generic List<int> stores ints directly, unlike ArrayList which boxes value types
List<int> numbers = [1, 2, 3];
Console.WriteLine($"Sum: {numbers.Sum()}");

interviewQuestions.answer:
Before generics, a collection designed to hold 'anything' typically used `object` as its element type (e.g. `ArrayList`). This works, but loses **compile-time type safety**: the compiler can't stop you from putting mismatched types into the same collection, and every read requires an explicit cast that can fail at runtime.

```csharp
ArrayList mixed = new ArrayList();
mixed.Add(42);
mixed.Add("oops"); // compiles fine - no type safety

foreach (object item in mixed)
{
    int n = (int)item; // throws InvalidCastException on "oops" at RUNTIME
}
```

With a generic collection, the type parameter is fixed once and enforced everywhere:

```csharp
List<int> numbers = new List<int>();
numbers.Add(42);
// numbers.Add("oops"); // compile-time error - caught immediately

foreach (int n in numbers)
{
    Console.WriteLine(n); // no cast needed, guaranteed to be an int
}
```

Beyond catching mistakes earlier (at compile time instead of in production), generics also remove the need for casts entirely at the call site, make the code more self-documenting (the type signature tells you exactly what's stored), and — for value types specifically — avoid the boxing overhead that `object`-based collections incur. This combination of safety, clarity, and performance is why generic collections are the default choice in virtually all modern C# code.
